      purpose: 'Learn GitHub Education eligibility and the official application route. Claim useful offers only when eligible and read the end date.',
      result: 'An application checklist and a plan for the offers you actually need.',
      sources: [links.students, links.pack],
      terms: [
        word('GitHub Education', 'GitHub’s education program for eligible students and educators.', 'Account settings → Education benefits', 'Apply with your own current student details.'),
        word('Student Developer Pack', 'A collection of developer offers for verified students.', 'education.github.com/pack', 'Each partner has its own redemption steps and conditions.'),
        word('Eligibility', 'The conditions you must meet to receive a benefit.', 'Currently enrolled + personal GitHub account + age 13 or older.', 'The official student rules determine whether you qualify.'),
        word('Enrollment proof', 'A document showing that you are currently a student.', 'Dated student ID, schedule, transcript, or enrollment letter.', 'GitHub lists acceptable evidence and may request additional details.'),
        word('Academic email', 'An email address provided by your school.', 'Your institution’s verified email domain.', 'Some schools require this in the application; an .edu ending is not a universal requirement.'),
        word('Redemption', 'Claiming an approved offer at its provider.', 'Open an offer from the Pack and follow its partner flow.', 'GitHub approval does not automatically activate every product.'),
        word('Credit', 'A limited amount a provider lets you spend on its service.', 'An offer has an amount and an expiry date.', 'A credit is different from unlimited free use.'),
        word('Renewal', 'Continuing a service after its first term ends.', 'A one-year domain reaches its renewal date.', 'Check the renewal price and automatic-renewal setting before claiming.')
      ],
      lessons: [
        lesson('qualify', 'Check the student requirements', 'Know whether and how you can apply.',
          ['GitHub’s current rules require a personal GitHub account, age 13 or older, and enrollment in a degree- or diploma-granting program. Examples include high school, college, university, or homeschool. You need evidence of current enrollment.', 'The application may require your school email, depending on your institution. If your school does not provide academic email, follow the official instructions for documenting that policy. Use your own truthful information.'],
          ['Eligibility', 'Enrollment proof', 'Academic email'], ['Open the official application guidance linked below.', 'Check age, account, and current enrollment requirements.', 'Gather a current dated ID, schedule, transcript, or enrollment letter.', 'Add and verify your academic email if the application requires it.'],
          [snippet('Your application checklist', 'text', 'My own personal GitHub account\nCurrent school and enrollment details\nAccepted current evidence\nVerified academic email if required by my application')],
          'You know what evidence to prepare, without guessing eligibility.', 'Write which evidence you can provide and what the application asks for.',
          'Does everyone learning to code automatically qualify?', ['Yes', 'No, GitHub verifies specific student requirements', 'Only people using Java qualify'], 1, 'Eligibility depends on student status and the official requirements.', 'Learning code and verified enrollment are separate facts.'),
        lesson('apply', 'Apply through GitHub and check the result', 'Use the official application process.',
          ['Go to your GitHub account’s Education benefits settings. Under GitHub Education, choose Start an application, complete the form, and submit it. Follow the current prompts rather than relying on screenshots from an older tutorial.', 'Review is not guaranteed to be instant or successful. Check your application status and any request for better evidence. Once approved, open the Education portal and Pack to access benefits.'],
          ['GitHub Education', 'Enrollment proof', 'Student Developer Pack'], ['Sign in to your own GitHub account.', 'Open account settings → Education benefits.', 'Start the application and upload evidence only through the official flow.', 'Check the outcome and follow any official correction instructions.'],
          [snippet('Official places', 'text', 'Application guidance: docs.github.com → GitHub Education → For students\nApplication: your GitHub Education benefits settings\nOffers: https://education.github.com/pack')],
          'You can locate the application and its status.', 'Find the official application page and read the requested fields before submitting.',
          'Where should you submit student evidence?', ['In a public repository', 'In the official GitHub Education application', 'In a stranger’s direct message'], 1, 'The official application is the intended verification channel.', 'Use the account’s education process.'),
        lesson('claim', 'Claim a useful offer and note its end date', 'Distinguish approval, redemption, credits, and renewal.',
          ['Open the current Pack page, choose an offer you need, and follow its partner link. Read region, age, first-account, card, usage, and expiration conditions that apply to that offer. Record when it ends and whether it renews at a paid price.', 'As checked on 2 October 2026, the Pack lists Namecheap domain registration for one year on the .me TLD. Availability and partner eligibility can change. A first-year domain offer does not promise free renewal. We use it as an optional route in the Domains subject.'],
          ['Redemption', 'Credit', 'Renewal', 'Student Developer Pack'], ['Choose one useful offer from the official live list.', 'Read the provider’s conditions before redeeming.', 'Record the start, end, limits, and renewal terms.', 'Avoid activating paid add-ons you do not need.'],
          [snippet('Offer notes · fill in from the provider', 'text', 'Provider:\nBenefit:\nWho qualifies:\nUsage limit:\nEnd date:\nRenewal price:\nAuto-renew setting:')],
          'A clear record of what is included and what happens afterward.', 'Fill in the offer notes for a domain or tool you are eligible to claim.',
          'What does a one-year free domain offer tell you about year two?', ['It is free forever', 'You must check renewal terms; year two may be paid', 'Domains never expire'], 1, 'The initial benefit and later renewal are different terms.', 'Read the offer’s end date and renewal price.')
      ]
